{
  "$schema": "../../catalog/skill.spec.schema.json",
  "id": "dab-rest-and-graphql",
  "domain": "apis-and-application-patterns",
  "value": [
    "not-in-training-data",
    "multi-step-with-silent-failures",
    "corrects-a-wrong-assumption"
  ],
  "correction": "Agents write a Data API builder configuration in its 1.x shape, one hand-authored entity block per table, and reason about it as though an unconfigured role is denied, when version 2.0 changed all three inputs to that judgement: dab init now writes the Unauthenticated provider so every request is evaluated as anonymous, a permission granted to anonymous is inherited upward by authenticated and by every unconfigured named role, and autoentities generate entities from T-SQL LIKE patterns whose default include is every object in every schema. The result is a configuration that starts cleanly, passes dab validate, and publishes more of the database to unauthenticated callers than the author asked for, with nothing in the startup output naming the excess.",
  "value_probe": {
    "gate": "The answer settles the question from what the running configuration actually exposes, reaching both which authentication provider was written into it and how a permission granted to one role reaches other roles, rather than from the permissions the author meant to grant.",
    "task": "I generated a configuration for the tool that serves REST and GraphQL straight over a database, pointed it at our Azure SQL Database, and it started with no complaints and passes its own validation. Our security reviewer wants me to prove which callers can read which tables before this goes anywhere near production. I granted read on one entity to one named role and nothing else. Can I tell him everyone else is denied, and how exactly would I check that claim?",
    "states": [
      "A permission granted to the anonymous role is inherited upward by authenticated and by every unconfigured named role.",
      "The provider written by the initialisation command can cause every request to be evaluated as anonymous.",
      "Generating entities from a pattern can include every object in every schema unless the pattern is narrowed.",
      "Nothing in the startup output names what ended up exposed, so the configuration itself has to be read."
    ],
    "refutes": [
      "A role that is not listed in an entity's permissions is denied access to that entity.",
      "A generated configuration requires authentication by default, so unauthenticated callers are refused.",
      "Passing the tool's own validation shows that only the intended roles can read the intended entities.",
      "Configuration written for the earlier major version behaves the same way under the current one."
    ]
  },
  "posture": [
    "read",
    "write",
    "execute"
  ],
  "maturity": "draft",
  "applies_to": [
    "azure-sql-db"
  ],
  "related": [
    "azuresql-db-dab",
    "build-app-on-azure-sql",
    "azure-functions-sql-bindings",
    "connect-to-azure-sql",
    "entra-id-auth",
    "deploy-app-to-azure",
    "provision-azure-sql-db",
    "data-api-clients-js-python"
  ],
  "validation": {
    "target": "both",
    "setup": [
      "an Azure SQL Database with at least two schemas, one of which holds a table that must not be published",
      "the Data API builder CLI at 2.0 or later, installed as a dotnet tool"
    ],
    "assert": [
      "dab auto-config-simulate is run before dab start, and its match list is compared against the tables the author intended",
      "the include and exclude patterns are T-SQL LIKE, so a pattern written as a regular expression matches nothing or the wrong set",
      "dab configure --show-effective-permissions reports the role that actually reaches each entity after inheritance, not the roles written in the file",
      "a configuration generated by dab init carries provider Unauthenticated, and is changed before the API is reachable from outside the developer machine",
      "the connection string in the deployed configuration carries no password and resolves through an environment variable or a key vault reference"
    ],
    "probes": [
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "dab --version | grep -q '^Microsoft.DataApiBuilder 2\\.'",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Every command in this skill is a dab command and every claim below is a 2.x claim, so the CLI being installed and reporting a 2.x build is the precondition for all of them. Measured 2026-09-03: exit 0, 2.0.9+17ae3aa8414514918b627c777026efbc135a57e9. A 1.x build here means the four version 2.0 facts in the body do not apply."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "dab auto-config --help | tr -s ' \\n' ' ' | grep -qF \"T-SQL LIKE pattern(s) to include database objects. Space-separated array of patterns. Default: '%.%'.\"",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The headline correction, taken from the tool's own help rather than from Learn: the include patterns are T-SQL LIKE and not regular expressions, and omitting them means every object in every schema. Measured 2026-09-03 on 2.0.9. If a future build makes the default conservative, or renames the syntax, the skill's central warning stops being true and this probe goes red first."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "dab init --help | tr -s ' \\n' ' ' | grep -qF 'mcp.enabled (Default: true)'",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Fact 4 of the body, that a second endpoint is published over the same entities and permissions whether or not anyone asked for it, sourced from the CLI's own help. Measured 2026-09-03 on 2.0.9. This is also the boundary probe: the endpoint exists by default, and everything about pointing a client at it belongs to azuresql-db-dab."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string \"@env('SQL_CONNECTION_STRING')\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && grep -q '\"provider\": \"Unauthenticated\"' dab-config.json && grep -q '\"mode\": \"production\"' dab-config.json",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Fact 1, made executable: a config generated by dab init with no auth flags evaluates every request as anonymous, and it does so while calling itself production. Measured 2026-09-03 on 2.0.9, both strings present in the generated file. This probe needs no database because dab init never opens a connection."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string \"@env('SQL_CONNECTION_STRING')\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && dab add Book --source dbo.books --source.type table --permissions 'anonymous:read' >/dev/null 2>&1 && dab configure --show-effective-permissions 2>&1 | grep -qF 'Role: authenticated | Actions: Read (inherited from: anonymous)'",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "Fact 2, upward role inheritance, which is the reason the skill tells a reader never to grant anonymous as a convenience. A single anonymous:read grant makes the CLI report authenticated as also holding Read, inherited. Measured 2026-09-03 on 2.0.9, that exact line. If inheritance is ever reversed or removed, the skill's central security advice becomes wrong and this fails."
      },
      {
        "where": "host",
        "cmd": "d=$(mktemp -d) && cd \"$d\" && dab init --database-type mssql --connection-string \"@env('SQL_CONNECTION_STRING')\" >/dev/null 2>&1 && dab add Order --source dbo.orders --source.type table --permissions 'consumer:create' --policy-database '@item.ownerId eq @claims.oid' >/dev/null 2>&1 && grep -q '\"database\": \"@item.ownerId eq @claims.oid\"' dab-config.json",
        "expect": {
          "succeeds": true
        },
        "why": "The silent failure the body warns about. Microsoft Learn states database policies are supported on read, update and delete only, because an INSERT takes no WHERE predicate. Measured 2026-09-03 on 2.0.9: the CLI accepts the policy on a create action, writes it into the config and exits 0 with no warning, so the author is left believing a filter is in force that the engine will never apply. This probe deliberately asserts the acceptance, and it should be inverted the day the CLI starts refusing it."
      }
    ]
  },
  "triggering": {
    "explicit": [
      "Use the dab-rest-and-graphql skill"
    ],
    "implicit": [
      "my dab-config.json has three hundred entities and I cannot maintain it",
      "why can anonymous callers read this entity when I only granted the reader role",
      "generate my Data API builder entities from a pattern instead of one block per table",
      "restrict which rows a caller can see in my Data API builder API",
      "move my Data API builder config to Azure SQL Database without a password in it"
    ],
    "routing": [
      "how do I add relationships to my Data API builder config",
      "my Data API builder API is serving tables I never listed",
      "which role can actually reach this entity after inheritance",
      "what does autoentities include by default"
    ],
    "negative": [
      "run Data API builder against my local Azure SQL Database container",
      "give me an API for this database",
      "set up Data API builder and show me a REST endpoint returning rows",
      "connect the Data API builder MCP endpoint to my agent",
      "add a SQL output binding to my Azure Functions app",
      "write an Entity Framework Core model for these tables",
      "scaffold a Next.js app with a database"
    ]
  }
}
